# Responsible AI Multi-Agent Architecture for Autonomous Vehicles

## Overview
This project proposes a safety-oriented multi-agent architecture for autonomous vehicle systems, with a focus on simulation-time monitoring, uncertainty handling, and fail-safe decision support.

## Problem
Autonomous systems must handle uncertainty in perception and decision-making. A safety architecture is needed to monitor confidence, detect risky states, and trigger safer fallback behavior when uncertainty is high.

## Approach
I designed a conceptual multi-agent system that:
- monitors perception confidence in real time
- identifies uncertain or risky conditions
- triggers fail-safe override behavior
- embeds safety logic directly into the decision pipeline

## Technical Themes
- autonomous systems
- simulation-driven validation
- uncertainty-aware decision support
- AI safety architecture

## Files
- `RAI-Multi-Agent_Autonomous.pdf` — project paper/writeup

## Key Takeaways
- Explored how safety and uncertainty can be integrated into AV system design
- Framed AI safety as a systems problem rather than a purely policy problem
- Built a project directly relevant to simulation, validation, and AV reliability

## Notes
This project originated in an AI ethics context, but it should be understood as a technical systems architecture for safety-aware autonomous behavior under uncertainty.
